  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` with a custom-GLSL water surface under a gradient sky dome. It passes `typecheck`. I couldn't run it in a browser, so the rendered frame and shader compilation are unchecked. I copied the setup from the Aura3D water recipe in the docs.\n\n- **Water:** the surface is a 240×240 grid whose spacing is tighter near the camera. The vertex shader displaces it with four directional sine waves driven by a `waveClock` uniform. The same waves also give an analytic normal.\n- **Fragment shader:**\n  - Value-noise ripples perturb the normal and fade out with distance.\n  - Fresnel (Schlick) mixes the body colour with the reflected sky.\n  - A Blinn-Phong sun glint sits on the perturbed normal.\n  - Foam shows on the crests.\n  - The far edge hazes into the horizon colour so it meets the sky.\n- **Sky:** a 600-unit sphere with its own custom shader draws a horizon-to-zenith gradient, a sun glow and a sun disc. The water uses the same sky function for its reflection, so the two match.\n- **Colour:** both shaders write linear light, with colours decoded from sRGB. The app sets `output: { toneMapping: \"none\" }` as the docs recommend for custom shaders, so the sky should not come out grey and the glint clips instead of being compressed.\n- **Camera and timing:** the camera is at height 3, looking toward the horizon with the sun in view. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set two animation frames after `app.ready()`.\n\nOne thing I did not confirm: `waveClock` is read in both the vertex and fragment stages. The docs say custom uniforms are auto-declared in GLSL, and the recipe only uses it in the vertex stage. If the fragment stage doesn't get it, the water shader will fail to compile and that draw is skipped silently.\n\n",
